put('embodiedqa',title='Embodied Question Answering',authors=['Abhishek Das','Samyak Datta','Georgia Gkioxari','Stefan Lee','Devi Parikh','Dhruv Batra'],year=2018,venue='CVPR 2018',pages=['3–8'],problem='An agent must move through a previously unseen House3D home to obtain visual evidence and answer a question. EQA v1 deliberately restricts questions to one target object, enabling shortest-path demonstrations. It is active visual QA, not natural-language route following.',method='EQA v1 filters SUNCG scenes and generates functional-program questions, excluding answer distributions with normalized entropy below 0.5 and questions seen in fewer than four environments. It has 4,246/506/529 train/validation/test questions in disjoint 643/67/57 homes. Four evaluated types are location, color, color-in-room, and spatial preposition. A four-block CNN is pretrained for RGB reconstruction, semantic segmentation, and depth. Separate two-layer 128-dimensional question LSTMs serve navigation and answering. PACMAN uses a recurrent planner choosing forward/left/right/stop and an MLP controller repeating an action up to five times before returning control. Actions move at most 0.25 m or turn 9 degrees. The answer head attends over only the last five frames and predicts one of 172 answers. Supervised navigation cross-entropy trains 15 epochs under a distance curriculum; QA trains 50 epochs. REINFORCE then fine-tunes the navigator with the answerer frozen, using a terminal correct-answer reward of 5 and forward-distance shaping coefficient 0.005. Evaluation starts 10, 30, or 50 actions before the goal and caps episodes at 100 actions.',novelty='Introduces embodied active QA and a planner/controller temporal hierarchy, combining existing CNN/RNN, imitation, and policy-gradient ingredients. The main distinction from passive VQA is control over observations; unlike IQA, object manipulation is not required.',results='PAPER EXPERIMENT: Table 1 at T−30, imitation PACMAN ends 1.50 m from target with QA mean rank 3.13; RL ends 2.19 m away with improved mean rank 2.99 and stops in only 32% of episodes. Authors explicitly observe overshooting. The shortest-path QA oracle has mean rank 3.21, showing the closest endpoint is not necessarily the most informative view. The question-conditioned LSTM ends at 2.23 m, supporting the hierarchy for navigation, but the small QA mean-rank changes should not be presented as high accuracy. Human navigation uses a subset and a different answer output, so there is no directly comparable human mean rank.',implementation=pending,bottlenecks=[b('eqa-stop','Answer-seeking reinforcement can improve visual evidence while overshooting the target and failing to stop.','A useful object view is encountered before the final movement decision.','The navigator is rewarded for answer correctness and progress while the answerer can attend to recent frames; terminal location and evidence quality differ.','AUTHOR ACKNOWLEDGEMENT and PAPER EXPERIMENT: Table 1 and §5.',['Explore until Confident','IQA'],'Partially addressed'),b('eqa-memory','Five-frame answering context cannot preserve arbitrary evidence collected much earlier.','Questions requiring distant-object comparison or long search.','The answerer discards all but the last five frames; evaluated v1 questions have one target.','METHOD: §4; broader question types are proposed rather than evaluated.',['IQA','VSI-Bench','OmniAgent'],'Partially addressed')],sources=[dict(url='https://embodiedqa.org/',locator='Official project')],limits=['Separate model/data-generation supplement and original House3D asset availability require verification.'])

put('iqa',title='IQA: Visual Question Answering in Interactive Environments',authors=['Daniel Gordon','Aniruddha Kembhavi','Mohammad Rastegari','Joseph Redmon','Dieter Fox','Ali Farhadi'],year=2018,venue='CVPR 2018',pages=['2–8'],problem='Answer existence, counting, and spatial-relation questions by navigating and opening/closing containers in AI2-THOR. The floor is discretized, localization is supplied, and objects must be visible and close enough for interaction. IQUAD has 76,800 training and 1,920 test configurations across 25 and five kitchen rooms.',method='HIMN factorizes a high-level GRU planner and low-level Navigator, Scanner, Detector, Manipulator, and Answerer. A 32-action high-level policy selects relative waypoints in a 5×5 grid, view rotations, interactions, or answer. Semantic spatial memory stores detection probabilities, occupancy, coverage, and visit intent. An egocentric spatial GRU updates only the current local window in a global tensor; other locations persist. A* replans over predicted occupancy while a supervised navigation head predicts feasibility/termination. YOLOv3 fine-tuned on training scenes and a depth network project objects into the map; scanning and manipulation controllers have fixed environment-defined behavior. The answerer convolves the full map with a tiled question vector, pools, and predicts the answer. Separate pretraining uses oracle planners/detections/partial maps where appropriate, followed by joint learning. A3C trains the planner with answer, exploration coverage, invalid-action and time rewards; an auxiliary supervised loss predicts validity of all actions.',novelty='Extends embodied QA to actions that change visibility and object state, and introduces local writes into persistent global semantic memory. Memory and control factorization are central; there is no VLM backbone or zero-training claim.',results='PAPER EXPERIMENT: Table 2 HIMN with YOLO reaches 68.47% existence, 30.43% counting, 58.67% spatial accuracy versus chance 50/25/50. Episodes average 318/926/516 actions, much longer than the small human sample. Table 3 oracle detection raises existence to 86.56 and spatial to 70.94; oracle navigation further raises counting to 48.44. Removing question input to the planner or the auxiliary validity loss returns performance near chance. Thus semantics and affordance supervision both matter. Table 5 explicitly separates novel arrangements in seen rooms from five unseen rooms. §5.5 acknowledges that the 2-D map cannot distinguish inside from on top and exploration revisits are inefficient.',implementation=pending,bottlenecks=[b('iqa-height','A 2-D semantic map aliases vertical containment and support relations.','Objects inside versus above the same container footprint.','Map projection removes height and explicit relations.','AUTHOR ACKNOWLEDGEMENT: §5.5.',['VSI-Bench','3-D map methods'],'Partially addressed'),b('iqa-count','Accurate global counting requires reliable persistent detections and efficient coverage.','Long searches with repeated object observations and hidden containers.','Perception errors and inefficient exploration accumulate before answering.','PAPER EXPERIMENT: Tables 2–3 counting and oracle gaps.',['Explore until Confident'],'Partially addressed')],limits=['Separate supplement and official implementation audit pending; no physical-robot evidence in the reviewed experiments.'])

put('embodiedgap',title='Rethinking the Embodied Gap in Vision-and-Language Navigation: A Holistic Study of Physical and Visual Disparities',authors=['Liuyi Wang','Xinyuan Xia','Hui Zhao','Hanqing Wang','Tai Wang','Yilun Chen','Chengju Liu','Qijun Chen','Jiangmiao Pang'],year=2025,venue='ICCV 2025',pages=['3–8'],problem='VLN-CE movement abstractions omit locomotion, falls, controller effects, camera differences, and lighting. VLN-PE evaluates ego-centric agents in physically simulated humanoid, quadruped, and wheeled embodiments using GRUtopia/Isaac Sim.',method='Ninety MP3D scenes are converted to USD and mesh holes manually repaired. Stairs are removed because controllers cannot reliably negotiate them, leaving 8,679/658/1,347 R2R train/seen/unseen episodes. Ten synthetic GRScenes and one Gaussian-splat laboratory add rendering/domain conditions. Baselines include Seq2Seq, CMA, NaVid, an adapted VLMaps with frontier exploration, and the new RDP. RDP encodes RGB/text with LongCLIP and depth with ResNet-50, uses cross-modal attention and GRU memory, and predicts eight relative (delta x, delta y, delta yaw) actions with a Transformer DDPM head. Noise-prediction MSE plus stop-progress MSE weighted 10 trains it. A separate stop head is necessary because diffusion actions do not reliably imply stopping. Metrics add fall and stuck rates to navigation success/path efficiency; the map-based comparison uses only 200 sampled episodes.',novelty='Primarily a platform and controlled transfer study, plus a recurrent diffusion-action baseline. Its physical simulation is more realistic than graph navigation or idealized CE, but does not itself prove real-robot reliability. Data collected with different controllers and embodiments test concrete sources of transfer error.',results='PAPER EXPERIMENT: Table 1 NaVid zero-shot H1 unseen SR/SPL 22.42/18.58; RDP trained in VLN-PE 21.98/16.44; fine-tuned CMA 23.31/19.66. These compare different data/parameter budgets. Table 4 controller-consistent CMA training/evaluation gives 18.78 SR versus 13.51 when trained without locomotion but tested with it; falls remain 18.63%. Table 5 adding all embodiments and CE data raises H1 SR 18.78→26.87 but confounds diversity and data amount. Table 6 NaVid SR falls 22.42→9.95 under dim light, while RGB-D RDP is stable near 22%; modality differs. Authors report NaVid rotating over 25 steps near goals in 70% of episodes. Gaussian-lab NaVid SR 5.81 is an observed failure, while rendering-noise attribution is their hypothesis. The study exposes concrete embodiment/stopping fragility while excluding stairs.',implementation=pending,bottlenecks=[b('vlnpe-physical','Navigation success does not transfer across controller and sensor conditions without substantial degradation.','Legged locomotion, changed camera height, dim lighting, or Gaussian-splat rendering.','Training observations and action consequences differ; depth availability and control stability vary by baseline.','PAPER EXPERIMENT: Tables 4–6.',['NaVILA','HumanoidVLN'],'Partially addressed'),b('vlnpe-stairs','Stair traversal remains outside the evaluated platform split.','Multi-floor routes requiring reliable legged stair control.','Controller failure led to filtering stair episodes.','AUTHOR ACKNOWLEDGEMENT: §3 datasets.',['HumanoidVLN'],'Still unresolved in the reviewed literature','Implementation and evaluation')],sources=[dict(url='https://crystalsixone.github.io/vln_pe.github.io/',locator='Official project'),dict(url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.13019',locator='Author manuscript')],limits=['Associated supplement and current InternNav implementation require version-mapping audit.'])

put('walkreadless',title='Walk and Read Less: Improving the Efficiency of Vision-and-Language Navigation via Tuning-Free Multimodal Token Pruning',authors=['Wenda Qin','Andrea Burns','Bryan A. Plummer','Margrit Betke'],year=2025,venue='EMNLP 2025, pp. 23556–23570',pages=['1','3–9','13–15'],problem='Removing visual/text tokens can lower per-step compute while increasing navigation length and removing critical action options. NAP changes inference of existing trained HAMT, DUET, and GOAT agents without additional parameter tuning.',method='Background pruning retains all navigable action-view tokens and removes low column-summed attention background tokens at each visual layer. Backtracking pruning retains only top-scored unvisited graph frontier nodes from earlier steps, reducing both graph tokens and available recovery destinations. Vocabulary priority pruning first builds an offline training-lexicon dictionary using Llama 3 relevance judgments; at each language layer it preferentially removes dictionary-irrelevant tokens, with attention ranking filling or reducing the pruning quota. The LLM is not called during navigation. The cost metric sums instruction encoder FLOPs once and visual/cross-modal FLOPs over decision steps using thop. CE uses ETPNav and its waypoint predictor. No new loss or fine-tuning is required, but the base policy, feature encoders and waypoint predictor are pretrained.',novelty='Task-specific foreground protection and graph-action pruning improve the accuracy/compute frontier relative to generic token pruning. The vocabulary is a context-independent relevance heuristic, not instruction entailment or semantic-preservation certification.',results='PAPER EXPERIMENT: Table 2 GOAT at roughly 50% tokens has R2R SR 73.8 versus full 78.1 and FLOPs 55.6%; RxR 63.9 versus 69.6 and FLOPs 49.3%; REVERIE 49.6 versus 53.8 and FLOPs 48.3%. The gain is over other pruned baselines, not full-model accuracy. Table 3 RxR SPL 61.8→54.3, steps 8.2→9.0. CE Table 7 full 56.9 SR/8.8 steps→52.7/9.6 with FLOPs 62.4%; pruning does not always shorten motion. Appendix F Table 13 calls one and flight irrelevant in a stair instruction: this is a documented prompt output and an untested numerical-language risk, not measured failure frequency. FLOPs do not establish wall-clock latency on target hardware. Appendix Table 11 has different step counts from main Table 2, limiting direct cross-table interpretation.',implementation=pending,bottlenecks=[b('nap-semantics','Context-independent word pruning can remove quantities or relational words that distinguish routes.','Ordinal, count, or context-sensitive instructions under tight token budgets.','A vocabulary-level relevance label ignores sentence composition; attention importance is not semantic necessity.','AUTHOR EXAMPLE: Appendix F Table 13 labels one and flight irrelevant; DEDUCTION for route effects.',['NavNuances','GELA'],'Uncertain because evidence is insufficient'),b('nap-recovery','Deleting graph frontier options trades recovery access for compute and sometimes still increases motion.','The needed corrective route is a low-attention frontier or pruned observations cause uncertainty.','BTP changes the action set, not only an internal representation.','PAPER EXPERIMENT: Tables 3 and 7.',['Meta-Explore','ULN'],'Partially addressed')],sources=[dict(url='https://github.com/wdqin/VLN-NAP',locator='Official EMNLP implementation')],associated=['Embedded appendices A–F, pp. 13–15'],limits=['Implementation and exact pruning-index validation pending.'])

put('vlnmme',title='VLN-MME: Diagnosing MLLMs as Navigation Agents',authors=['Xunyi Zhao','Gengze Zhou','Qi Wu'],year=2026,venue='ACL 2026, pp. 28207–28231',pages=['3–9','12–25'],problem='Compare zero-shot MLLM backbones and prompt-based memory/reasoning designs using cached views instead of live rendering. The tasks cover R2R, REVERIE navigation without object grounding, and graph-adapted MP3D ObjectNav; physical control and multilingual/interactive dialogue are excluded.',method='A Model/Agent/Task interface and Runner load cached cardinal images, connectivity and caption metadata. Four 90-degree views are rearranged as left/front/right/back after heading quantization to the nearest cardinal direction. GPT-5 produces marker captions; BLIP-2 plus GPT-5 supplies viewpoint summaries. Two memory families use textual action summaries or an accumulating textual topological graph. Each has direct, CoT, reflection, and combined prompts. A parser extracts action identifiers and retries invalid outputs. No model parameter updates occur. Stratified subsets preserve scene/path distributions; evaluation uses standard endpoint and trajectory metrics. The cost comparison removes rendering and continuous waypoint prediction, so its speedup is framework-specific.',novelty='A modular simulator-free diagnostic evaluation and agent ablations, not a new continual-learning method. It makes failure inspection practical but keeps graph connectivity, cached views and generated caption inputs, which differ from physical sensors and from raw-video-only policies.',results='PAPER EXPERIMENT: Table 2 Qwen2.5-VL-7B text-summary R2R SR 27.5→21.0 with CoT; Gemini baseline 41.0→32.5. This is a result for specified prompts/models, not a theorem against reasoning. Appendix E analyzes 200 Qwen map-memory trajectories: 148 failures, including 17 generation errors and 131 navigation errors, of which 106 loop; 42/52 successes also loop. Only two are described as perfect. On 25 selected hard negatives, Qwen3VL-assisted navigation reaches 52% SR versus selected baseline 0%; few-shot failure examples reach 16%. Assistance changes available information and policy, so this does not causally establish perception is adequate and only planning fails. Appendix Table 6 contradicts a universal 2–3-point proxy claim: NaviLLM REVERIE SR 45→34 and SPL 37→27 on the subset. Appendix D.4 describes reflection revision, while D.8 says reflection/decision metadata do not directly alter control flow; source behavior must arbitrate.',implementation=pending,bottlenecks=[b('vlnmme-history','Provided textual history and topology do not reliably prevent repeated wrong actions.','Qwen map-memory agents on the inspected 200-episode subset and tested zero-shot prompt designs.','Models may fail to use history, interpret regions/verticality, or map recognition to actions; the evidence does not isolate these causes.','PAPER EXPERIMENT: Appendix E, Figures 13–14.',['M-Track','Meta-Explore','NavNuances'],'Partially addressed'),b('vlnmme-proxy','A small benchmark subset is not uniformly a close numerical proxy for every policy.','Comparing methods whose errors concentrate on selected scene/path categories.','Stratification on observed attributes does not control all policy-specific failure distributions.','PAPER EXPERIMENT: Appendix Table 6 REVERIE NaviLLM difference.',['NavNuances'],'Uncertain because evidence is insufficient','Evaluation')],sources=[dict(url='https://aclanthology.org/2026.acl-long.1300/',locator='Official proceedings'),dict(url='https://github.com/billzhao1030/VLN-MME',locator='Official implementation')],associated=['Embedded appendices A–F, pp. 12–25'],limits=['Figures 15–19 reviewed via text captions rather than full image-level trajectory verification. Source audit pending.'])

put('omniagent',title='Native Active Perception as Reasoning for Omni-Modal Understanding',authors=['Zhenghao Xing','Ruiyang Xu','Yuxuan Wang','Jinzheng He','Ziyang Ma','Qize Yang','Yunfei Chu','Jin Xu','Junyang Lin','Chi-Wing Fu','Pheng-Ann Heng'],year=2026,venue='ICML 2026 accepted, stated in author manuscript arXiv:2606.19341v2',pages=['1','3–9','13–20'],problem='Answer questions about long videos by selectively retrieving frames, audio or clips. The environment is a media archive with arbitrary temporal access, not a mobile robot or an unknown navigation scene.',method='Qwen2.5-Omni-7B follows an Observation/Thought/Action loop. At each turn it reads the previous transient media plus persistent textual history, writes a textual observation and reasoning record, and calls frame/audio/clip retrieval or answer. Previous raw media are removed while timestamps and textual summaries persist. Agentic SFT uses 58,000 successful teacher trajectories from five training datasets, filtered by task outcome and GPT-4o coherence score at least 3/5. Two SFT epochs use batch 64, lr 1e-5, 16 A100s. RL uses harder queries under 300 seconds, group eight, 150 steps, batch 256, lr 1e-6, 64 A100s. TAURA normalizes trajectory rewards and multiplies each turn advantage by its mean token entropy divided by group mean turn entropy. A clipped policy objective uses these rescaled advantages, with no KL or entropy regularizer. At inference context is 64K; the prompt requests confidence at least 0.9 before answering but forces a best answer at the final step.',novelty='Native multimodal active retrieval plus textual memory consolidation and entropy-weighted RL extends agentic video reasoning. Active perception is established in EmbodiedQA/IQA/Explore-EQA; this method instead exploits nonphysical media access. Entropy-correlated decision forks are not causal information gain, and prompt confidence is not calibrated uncertainty.',results='PAPER EXPERIMENT: Table 1 VSI-Bench 35.5→48.4 and LVBench 43.0→50.5 versus the base model, with additional SFT/RL data and inference. Table 4 LVBench agentic SFT 48.7, vanilla GRPO 49.8, TAURA 50.5; DailyOmni 63.3/62.2/64.8. Appendix C’s 79.2% high-entropy fork statistic uses Gemini-labeled top forks on successful traces, without a causal intervention or failure-sample control. Table 7 on 100 LVBench cases: base 34.8 seconds/41% accuracy; OmniAgent 66.8 seconds/51%, despite fewer frames than a 72B comparator. Memory media size is bounded per turn, but textual history and serial latency grow with turns. DEDUCTION: mean turn weights equal one, yet unequal token counts, signed advantages and gradient directions mean this does not prove preservation of original gradient magnitude. No benchmark or gradient test was executed here.',implementation=pending,bottlenecks=[b('omni-memory','Purging raw media makes later reasoning dependent on the faithfulness and completeness of textual observations.','An important detail was omitted or misdescribed, or a later question changes which information matters.','Only selected textual summaries survive; recovering evidence requires another archive query.','METHOD: Appendix B.4 and prompt warning; DEDUCTION for irrecoverable physical observations.',['VSI-Bench','Explore until Confident'],'Partially addressed'),b('omni-credit','Entropy weighting is a useful heuristic but is not established causal credit assignment.','High-entropy filler or confidently wrong pivotal decisions.','Outcome-level advantages are redistributed by entropy rather than an intervention on action value.','PAPER EXPERIMENT: Table 4; limited correlation audit Appendix C.',['UoT'],'Uncertain because evidence is insufficient')],sources=[dict(url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.19341',locator='Author manuscript v2, 17 July 2026'),dict(url='https://github.com/harryhsing/OmniAgent',locator='Official implementation')],associated=['Embedded appendices A–D, pp. 13–20'],limits=['Official proceedings verification and released training/source audit pending. No causal or benchmark validation performed.'])

put('uot',title='Uncertainty of Thoughts: Uncertainty-Aware Planning Enhances Information Seeking in LLMs',authors=['Zhiyuan Hu','Chumin Liu','Xidong Feng','Yilun Zhao','See-Kiong Ng','Anh Tuan Luu','Junxian He','Pang Wei Koh','Bryan Hooi'],year=2024,venue='NeurIPS 2024',pages=['1','3–10','14–20','24–26'],problem='Select useful follow-up questions when the correct answer is hidden. Evaluations are simulated 20 Questions, simplified diagnosis, and troubleshooting, not clinical deployment or embodied navigation.',method='An LLM proposes candidate questions from dialogue history and a candidate hypothesis set. Each simulated answer partitions hypotheses into disjoint yes/no sets; experiments assume a uniform prior. Up to three question/answer levels form a tree. Binary answer entropy is divided by 1+abs(p_yes−p_no)/lambda (lambda 0.4 in the plotted reward) to favor balanced partitions. Rewards accumulate from root to leaf; expectation backs up using answer probabilities and uniform averaging over future generated questions. The current root question with highest backed-up score is asked. Thus future question nodes average candidates rather than maximizing a Bellman-optimal continuation. Closed-set evaluation provides the hypothesis list; open-set mode uses LLM-generated lists of 5–10 candidates and refreshes them, with three initial direct-prompt turns for 20 Questions. No model fine-tuning occurs, but repeated model calls supply simulation and a GPT-4 answerer supplies environmental responses.',novelty='Information-theoretic question scoring and lookahead replace unconstrained self-evaluation in tree-style prompting. The underlying information-gain identity is standard for deterministic partitions; the sharpened reward is a heuristic and is not identical to Shannon information gain.',results='PAPER EXPERIMENT: Table 1 GPT-4 closed-set Common SR 50.5→71.2, DX 91.3→97.0, FloDial 43.7→67.3. Open-set Common SR 48.6→55.3 while mean dialogue length slightly increases 17.1→17.4; improvement is not universal efficiency. Table 2 equal-token comparisons give pruned UoT 4.7K tokens and 20Q SR 48.1 versus adapted ToT 4.5K/33.8; full UoT 9.2K/54.4. Appendix F human audits only 10% of simulated records, finding intermediate feedback accuracy 92.9–94.4%, so simulator truth is imperfect. §5 explicitly acknowledges overlapping/noisy hypotheses and incomplete elimination. The printed Eqs. 5–6 omit entropy minus signs; Appendix A restores the intended negative entropy but an intermediate displayed algebra step drops probability factors. The final binary-entropy identity is valid under deterministic disjoint partitions; these typesetting inconsistencies should not be treated as an invalid identity.',implementation=pending,bottlenecks=[b('uot-elimination','Hard hypothesis deletion is unreliable when answers are noisy, ambiguous, or compatible with several hypotheses.','A symptom is absent despite a disease, or LLM simulated partitions are wrong.','Yes/no sets are assumed disjoint and exhaustive; uncertain likelihoods are replaced by elimination.','AUTHOR ACKNOWLEDGEMENT: §5; PAPER EXPERIMENT: Appendix F imperfect feedback.',['KnowNo','AIUTA'],'Still unresolved in the reviewed literature'),b('uot-search','Tree lookahead spends many model calls and optimizes a heuristic average continuation.','Larger candidate spaces, richer answer types, deeper planning.','Branching grows with questions and answers, and the backed-up reward averages future choices.','METHOD: §2.5; PAPER EXPERIMENT: Table 2 and Appendix E.',['OmniAgent'],'Partially addressed')],sources=[dict(url='https://github.com/zhiyuanhubj/UoT',locator='Official implementation')],associated=['Embedded appendices A–I partly reviewed and selected prompts, precise pages listed above'],limits=['Remaining prompt/setup pages and source audit pending. No claim about medical safety or clinical utility.'])

put('cot',title='Chain-of-Thought Prompting Elicits Reasoning in Large Language Models',authors=['Jason Wei','Xuezhi Wang','Dale Schuurmans','Maarten Bosma','Brian Ichter','Fei Xia','Ed H. Chi','Quoc V. Le','Denny Zhou'],year=2022,venue='NeurIPS 2022',pages=['2–9','15–17'],problem='Improve multi-step arithmetic, commonsense, and symbolic problem solving with a pretrained language model and a few demonstrations, without task fine-tuning. This is foundational prompting research, not a VLN architecture.',method='Replace input/answer exemplars by input/intermediate natural-language rationale/answer triples. The model autoregressively generates intermediate tokens before its answer. Eight manually composed arithmetic demonstrations are reused across free-response datasets; AQuA uses four multiple-choice examples. Commonsense tasks use task-specific training examples, or hold out the first ten examples of evaluation-only BIG-bench tasks for prompts. Greedy decoding is used; LaMDA averages five exemplar-order seeds, while other model results use one ordering. No loss or training update is introduced. Ablations test equation-only output, dots as extra computation, and explanations after the answer. Symbolic tasks test two-step exemplars followed by longer inputs. SayCan is evaluated as language-to-action sequence generation rather than physical execution in this paper.',novelty='Combines pretrained in-context learning with intermediate natural-language rationales, contrasting earlier rationale-supervised training and scratchpad/program approaches. It does not establish that a verbal rationale faithfully reports all internal computation or that longer text always improves a decision.',results='PAPER EXPERIMENT: Figure 2 PaLM 540B GSM8K solve rate roughly 18→57%; StrategyQA reaches 75.6 versus prior best 69.4. Benefits vary by scale and task; one-operation arithmetic gains are small or negative, and small models can produce fluent incorrect traces. Of 50 correct LaMDA answers manually inspected, two had invalid rationales; of 50 errors, 46% were described as nearly correct and 54% as major semantic/coherence errors. Appendix A explicitly notes parameter count is confounded with training compute. Dot and post-answer controls support usefulness of intermediate content in these setups, but do not isolate all possible extra-compute mechanisms. §6 explicitly disclaims guaranteed correct reasoning and leaves whether the network reasons as an open question.',implementation='Prompts and reported outputs are supplied in appendices; original models include proprietary LaMDA and PaLM. Official supplementary release/code discovery pending. No model execution.',bottlenecks=[b('cot-faithfulness','A correct final answer does not guarantee a correct or faithful intermediate rationale.','Fluent semantic errors, lucky answers, or explanations that fail to reflect the causal computation.','Autoregressive text is not a verified derivation and no correctness constraint is imposed.','AUTHOR ACKNOWLEDGEMENT: §6; manual 2/50 counterexamples in §3.2.',['VSI-Bench','VLN-MME','OmniAgent'],'Partially addressed'),b('cot-transfer','Reasoning-prompt gains do not automatically transfer to grounded sequential navigation.','An agent must collect observations, interpret geometry, and execute actions over time.','Original evidence is primarily text reasoning with fixed inputs.','DEDUCTION from task scope.',['VLN-MME','VSI-Bench','UoT'],'Partially addressed')],sources=[dict(url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.11903',locator='Author manuscript')],limits=['Detailed output/prompt appendix pages and available code artifacts remain to be reviewed. Historical API versions limit direct reproducibility.'])

put('landmark_rxr',title='Landmark-RxR: Solving Vision-and-Language Navigation with Fine-Grained Alignment Supervision',authors=['Keji He','Yan Huang','Qi Wu','Jianhua Yang','Dong An','Shuanglin Sima','Liang Wang'],year=2021,venue='NeurIPS 2021',pages=['1','3–9'],problem='Coarse instruction/trajectory pairs weakly supervise individual landmarks; trajectory metrics can be dominated by where a difficult segment occurs. Landmark-RxR supplies human sub-instruction and sub-trajectory boundaries and a diagnostic reset protocol.',method='Thirty annotators spend about 2,700 hours producing 166,740 sub-instructions and 46,645 sub-trajectories from English RxR. Each chunk has independent meaning, a changed position and clear endpoint, with cross-annotator verification. Sub-pairs, recombined adjacent pairs, full English RxR and R2R train an RCM-style encoder-decoder with ResNet panoramas and GloVe-300. Student-forcing imitation runs 100K steps, then policy-gradient training 20K, batch 100, lr 1e-4. Soft focal reward weights DTW costs at two sampled annotated critical landmarks by ten before exponentiation; hard focal reward sums +1/−1 for whether any predicted point visits each selected landmark within 3 m. The latter is order-insensitive. Diagnostic evaluation allows ten steps per landmark, then resets the agent to a failed annotated landmark and gives only the unfinished instruction suffix. This is oracle intervention, not autonomous recovery.',novelty='Human alignment supervision and landmark-focused rewards complement global route similarity. The reset metrics isolate local competence and must be reported separately from ordinary complete-route navigation. Hard landmark reward precedes any proposal simply rewarding intermediate landmark coverage.',results='PAPER EXPERIMENT: Table 3 all-granularity imitation SR/nDTW 28.4/26.7; nDTW RL 29.3/43.2; soft focal 33.7/41.0; hard focal 34.9/36.6 on English RxR unseen. Thus SR and global alignment trade off. Table 2 adding fine-grained RxR pairs to R2R raises SR 33.0→40.1 while adding full pairs alone yields 33.1. Extra annotations, chunk lengths, and data volume all change; this is not a pure architecture comparison. Table 4 reset-based loss number improves 6,256→4,951 with hard focal reward, but those reset counts do not measure autonomous recovery capability. The report’s attention illustration is supportive qualitative evidence, not a causal grounding proof.',implementation='Pinned official repository contains README and data links, with a supplementary PDF link; no model, optimizer, inference or evaluation source files are present in the inspected tree. Data-release provenance is established, but the paper’s code-availability statement is not fulfilled by model source at this inspected commit. Supplement review pending.',repo_status='Official repository pinned; dataset/README release only at inspected tree',bottlenecks=[b('landmark-order','Landmark visitation alone does not enforce ordered or procedural instruction constraints.','A path visits the right objects in the wrong order or repeats an action incorrectly.','Hard reward uses a minimum distance over all predicted points independently for each landmark.','DEDUCTION from Eqs. 3–4.',['M-Track','NavNuances'],'Partially addressed'),b('landmark-reset','Reset-based local success does not quantify an agent’s ability to recover by itself.','A route fails before a later annotated segment.','Evaluation supplies the correct landmark location and remaining instruction after failure.','METHOD: §4.',['Meta-Explore','ULN'],'Uncertain because evidence is insufficient','Evaluation')],sources=[dict(url='https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper/2021/file/0602940f23884f782058efac46f64b0f-Paper.pdf',locator='Official main paper'),dict(url='https://github.com/hekj/Landmark-RxR',locator='Official dataset release')],limits=['Repository tree/README inspection is not model-code inspection; no released model source found at pinned commit.'])

put('mtrack',title='M-Track: Milestone-Aware Reinforcement Learning for Visual Navigation',authors=['Chan Hee Song','Jihyung Kil','Tai-Yu Pan','Brian M. Sadler','Wei-Lun Chao','Yu Su'],year=2022,venue='CVPR 2022; exact title to verify against first page',pages=['3–8'],problem='Long ALFRED household tasks fail when an agent skips a subtask or interacts with the wrong object. M-Track explicitly tracks completion of supplied low-level instructions, in addition to a high-level goal.',method='A BERT-CRF trained on simulator-derived labels extracts milestone type and object targets. A Mask R-CNN predicts 116 object classes, masks and binary reachability within 1.5 m. WordNet WUP similarity grounds target names to reachable detections. Navigation milestones require a reachable target; interaction milestones additionally require the action mask to overlap it above IoU 0.5. A checklist tracks multiple target objects. The base LSTM or recurrent VLN-BERT sees the high-level goal and only the current low-level sentence, advancing after milestone completion. Before executing an interaction, proactive checking scans the top five predicted actions for a matching target, otherwise chooses navigation. Training combines action imitation, object selection and A2C rewards for distance, interaction correctness, visibility and final success. The VLN-BERT adaptation uses eight panoramic views and object features plus a pointer action head.',novelty='Explicit grounded progress checks and proactive action rejection, rather than implicit attention or a learned binary switch. It is model-agnostic but the evaluated checker assumes object-centered ALFRED milestones and provided instruction decomposition.',results='PAPER EXPERIMENT: Table 2 unseen LSTM-L SR/PLWSR 8.70/4.05→13.28/6.25; VLN-BERT-L 12.23/5.60→16.29/7.66. Table 3 applying M-Track only at test improves validation SR 10.35→13.29, while training and testing with it reaches 17.29. Table 4 ground-truth milestones reach 24.38, leaving extraction/grounding room. §6 explicitly states procedural instructions such as turning twice need action-history checks, implicit milestones require inference, and multiple valid subtask orders call for softer constraints. These limitations are direct prior-art statements for any proposed obligation/history tracker.',implementation='Assignment 05 is independently inspecting the official runtime milestone implementation; pinned paths and findings will be incorporated. Main paper personally reviewed here; no code execution.',bottlenecks=[b('mtrack-procedure','Object reachability and interaction checks do not certify procedural action-history requirements.','Turn twice, preserve order, or satisfy an implicit prerequisite that is not an object-state condition.','Current milestones are type/target tuples and the checker primarily observes current objects.','AUTHOR ACKNOWLEDGEMENT: §6.',['NavNuances','Landmark-RxR'],'Still unresolved in the reviewed literature'),b('mtrack-hard','Hard sequential milestones can block valid alternative task orders or propagate a wrong target extraction.','Multiple valid plans or implicit/ambiguous instructions.','Only the current sentence is exposed and all target checks must complete before advancing.','AUTHOR ACKNOWLEDGEMENT: §6; PAPER EXPERIMENT: Table 4 GT gap.',['FGR2R','BabyWalk'],'Partially addressed')],sources=[dict(url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.07028',locator='Author full version'),dict(url='https://github.com/chanhee-luke/M-Track',locator='Official implementation')],limits=['Exact bibliographic title and supplementary model details need final verification; implementation review delegated within existing assignment 05.'])

put('environment_review',title='Environment understanding for VLMs and VLAs (2020–2025)',authors=[],year=None,venue='Supplied internal research report; not a verified conference paper',pages=['1–13'],problem='A secondary landscape report organizes scene graphs, dynamic awareness, spatial reasoning, VLAs, efficiency and Korean/multilingual directions. It is useful as a discovery index, not primary evidence.',method='No original model, dataset, optimization, or experimental protocol is supplied. The report groups named methods and reproduces headline numbers with web-style source labels. Its claimed coverage is 2020–2025, while some text refers to 2026 models and events. Individual claims require checking the underlying primary paper/version; generic domain labels do not identify a precise supporting experiment.',novelty='No research novelty can be established for this report. Its taxonomy can aid folder organization and searches, but its gap assertions cannot certify absence of prior work.',results='AUDIT VERDICT: Do not use this report as evidence that environment understanding universally dominates model size, that NaVid eliminates sim-to-real gaps, that efficiency improvements generalize across setups, or that no Korean robot-instruction dataset exists. The NaVid claim conflicts with the directly reviewed VLN-PE transfer/lighting failures. Its VSI-Bench best-model number does not match the directly reviewed Table 1 under the same apparent scope. Broad claims about grounding versus scale confound encoders, training data, action heads and evaluation. These are reasons to verify specific claims, not a finding that every cited paper is incorrect.',implementation='Not applicable: no associated original implementation or experimental artifact is provided. Named repositories belong to separate papers and must be audited under their own identities.',repo_status='Not applicable: secondary internal report',bottlenecks=[b('report-evidence','Broad landscape claims lack an auditable primary-source chain and matched experimental scope.','Using the report to justify novelty, absence of prior work, or quantitative cross-paper rankings.','Headline summaries combine heterogeneous settings and sometimes stronger conclusions than the underlying methods support.','INSPECTED REPORT: pp. 1, 6, 10–13; direct cross-check against VLN-PE and VSI-Bench dossiers.',['Primary-paper dossiers and assignments 05–06'],'Uncertain because evidence is insufficient','Evidence quality')],limits=['Authorship, creation date, and source-link provenance are not established. No findings in this report are treated as original experimental evidence.'],status='Complete as an internal-report audit; primary cited literature is handled separately')
